(1, 'Apex Infra Projects Ltd. v. Bharat National Highways Authority [SYNTHETIC TEST BENCHMARK]', 'High Court of Delhi (Commercial Division)', '2023-04-18', '2023 SCC OnLine Del 1402 (Synthetic)', 'CS (COMM) 184/2023', 'Commercial Contracts & Arbitration', 'https://delhihighcourt.nic.in/case-sample/184-2023', NULL, 'The plaintiff entered into an EPC contract dated 12.01.2019 for the four-laning of Highway Stretch NH-48. Due to delays in handing over encumbrance-free right of way and environmental clearances, cost escalation claims arose. The defendant invoked the bank guarantee without adhering to the dispute resolution mechanism prescribed under Clause 26. Held, the pre-institution mediation mandate under Section 12A of the Commercial Courts Act 2015 is mandatory except where urgent interim relief is genuinely contemplated. Injunction against invocation of unconditional bank guarantee cannot be granted in the absence of egregious fraud or irretrievable injustice.', 'Plaintiff was awarded an EPC contract for Highway NH-48. Due to delayed site handover by the Authority, disputes arose over liquidated damages and price variation. Authority attempted encashment of Performance Bank Guarantee. Plaintiff filed commercial suit without pre-institution mediation.', '1. Whether compliance with Section 12A of the Commercial Courts Act 2015 (pre-institution mediation) is mandatory prior to instituting a commercial suit.\n2. Whether encashment of an unconditional bank guarantee can be restrained on grounds of breach of underlying contract.', NULL, 'The Commercial Courts Act was enacted to expedite commercial adjudications. Section 12A employs mandatory language (Patil Automation doctrine). Where urgent interim relief is genuinely sought, exemption may be claimed, but the court must scrutinize whether the plea of urgency is a facade. On bank guarantees, established jurisprudence dictates that unconditional guarantees constitute independent contracts between bank and beneficiary.', 'Pre-institution mediation under Section 12A is mandatory unless urgent interim relief is genuinely established. Restraint on encashment of unconditional bank guarantees requires established egregious fraud or irretrievable harm.', 'Application for injunction against bank guarantee dismissed. Parties referred to arbitral tribunal in accordance with Clause 26.', 'Commercial Courts Act 2015 (Section 12A, Section 2(1)(c)); Indian Contract Act 1872 (Section 126); Arbitration and Conciliation Act 1996 (Section 9, Section 11).', 'completed'),
(2, 'Zenith Global Technologies Pvt. Ltd. v. Horizon Telecom Services [SYNTHETIC TEST BENCHMARK]', 'High Court of Judicature at Bombay (Commercial Division)', '2023-08-22', '2023 Bom CR 512 (Synthetic)', 'Comm. Arb. Petition 89/2023', 'Arbitration & Award Enforcement', 'https://bombayhighcourt.nic.in/case-sample/89-2023', NULL, 'Petition under Section 34 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act 1996 challenging an arbitral award awarding damages for breach of Software Master Services Agreement. The petitioner contended patent illegality and breach of natural justice under Section 34(2)(b)(ii). The arbitral tribunal had correctly interpreted Clause 14 (limitation of liability) and applied the test of foreseeability under Section 73 of the Indian Contract Act. The court reiterated that Section 34 does not permit reappraisal of evidence or substitution of the tribunal plausible view.', 'Petitioner provided cloud billing software to respondent. Systems suffered outages leading to contract termination and claim for consequential damages. Sole arbitrator awarded liquidated sums. Petitioner challenged award alleging patent illegality on the face of the record.', '1. Does the arbitral award violate public policy or suffer from patent illegality under Section 34(2A)?\n2. Did the tribunal exceed its jurisdiction by awarding consequential damages beyond the limitation of liability cap?', NULL, 'The scope of judicial interference under Section 34 is well-circumscribed. Patent illegality must be such as goes to the root of the matter and cannot be a mere erroneous application of law. An arbitrator is the ultimate master of factual evidence. Construction of contract terms lies squarely within the arbitrators purview unless manifestly perverse.', 'A commercial court under Section 34 cannot act as an appellate court. An interpretation that is reasonable and plausible cannot be set aside merely because an alternative interpretation is possible.', 'Arbitration petition dismissed. Award upheld with post-award interest at 9% per annum.', 'Arbitration and Conciliation Act 1996 (Section 34, Section 31(7)); Indian Contract Act 1872 (Section 73, Section 74).', 'completed'),
(3, 'Sterling Logistics & Warehousing LLP v. Continental Shipping Corp [SYNTHETIC TEST BENCHMARK]', 'Supreme Court of India (Civil Appellate)', '2024-01-15', '(2024) 2 SCC 415 (Synthetic)', 'Civil Appeal No. 712/2024', 'Commercial Contracts & Specific Relief', 'https://main.sci.gov.in/case-sample/712-2024', NULL, 'Appeal against judgment vacating interim stay in a multi-modal logistics contract for multimodal container freight stations. Question concerned application of Section 20A and Section 41(ha) of the Specific Relief Act 1963 as amended in 2018. Held, statutory injunctions cannot be granted where the infrastructure project specified in the Schedule would be impeded or delayed. Logistics hubs and supply chain corridors fall under Category 4 of the Schedule to the Specific Relief Act.', 'Appellant entered concession agreement to build container freight terminal. Delay occurred due to land acquisition disputes. Respondent port authority terminated concession and invited fresh bids. Appellant sought injunction against termination.', '1. Whether multi-modal container freight terminals qualify as infrastructure projects under the Schedule to the Specific Relief Act 1963.\n2. Whether the bar under Section 20A applies to concession contracts undergoing dispute resolution.', NULL, 'The 2018 amendments to the Specific Relief Act reflect legislative intent to prioritize public infrastructure completion over civil disputes. Section 20A expressly debars courts from granting injunctions in suits involving infrastructure projects if granting the injunction would impede timely progress. Courts must balance commercial equities through damages rather than blocking public projects.', 'Courts cannot grant injunctions under Section 20A and Section 41(ha) in relation to infrastructure projects specified in the Schedule if such orders cause delay or impediment.', 'Appeal dismissed. Liberty granted to appellant to pursue liquidated damages through arbitration.', 'Specific Relief Act 1963 (Section 20A, Section 41(ha), Schedule); Commercial Courts Act 2015 (Section 10).', 'completed'),
(4, 'BioMedika Formulations Ltd. v. CureWell Pharma Innovations [SYNTHETIC TEST BENCHMARK]', 'High Court of Delhi (Intellectual Property Division)', '2023-11-09', '2023 SCC OnLine Del 3190 (Synthetic)', 'CS (COMM) 420/2023', 'Intellectual Property & Patents', 'https://delhihighcourt.nic.in/case-sample/420-2023', NULL, 'Suit for permanent injunction restraining patent infringement of an anti-diabetic crystalline composition (Patent No. IN 312891). The defendant contended invalidity under Section 3(d) of the Patents Act 1970 citing lack of enhanced therapeutic efficacy over prior art polymorphic forms. The court analyzed experimental data submitted by scientific experts and determined that the plaintiff established substantial bioavailability improvements satisfying the therapeutic efficacy threshold.', 'Plaintiff holds registered Indian Patent for crystalline salt formulation of an SGLT2 inhibitor. Defendant launched generic formulation before patent expiry alleging Section 3(d) invalidity as known substance derivative.', '1. Whether the crystalline formulation satisfies the test of significantly enhanced therapeutic efficacy under Section 3(d).\n2. Whether defendant demonstrated a credible challenge of obviousness and prior art anticipation.', NULL, 'Under Section 3(d), mere discovery of a new form of a known substance which does not result in the enhancement of the known efficacy of that substance is not patentable. However, in pharmaceuticals, therapeutic efficacy encompasses improved pharmacokinetic profiles resulting in lower required dosage and reduced toxicological side effects, as demonstrated in peer-reviewed clinical trials.', 'Enhanced therapeutic efficacy under Section 3(d) must be demonstrated through rigorous comparative bioavailability and clinical data. Where established, polymorph derivatives are validly patentable.', 'Interim injunction granted restraining manufacture and marketing of infringing generic formulation during pendency of trial.', 'Patents Act 1970 (Section 3(d), Section 48, Section 64, Section 107A); Commercial Courts Act 2015.', 'completed'),
(5, 'Orbit Retail Chains Ltd. v. Kuber Realty Ventures Pvt. Ltd. [SYNTHETIC TEST BENCHMARK]', 'High Court of Karnataka (Commercial Division)', '2024-02-14', '2024 (1) Kar LJ 288 (Synthetic)', 'Commercial Appeal 15/2024', 'Commercial Leases & IBC Interplay', 'https://karnatakahi.nic.in/case-sample/15-2024', NULL, 'Commercial dispute concerning eviction and recovery of unpaid rent for a flagship mall anchor store. The respondent landlord initiated proceedings before the Commercial Court. The appellant anchor tenant raised Section 14 moratorium under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code 2016 following admission of Section 9 application by NCLT. Held, Section 14(1)(d) bars recovery of any property by an owner or lessor where such property is occupied by or in the possession of the corporate debtor.', 'Anchor tenant of commercial shopping mall defaulted on lease rentals for 14 months. Landlord filed eviction suit in Commercial Court. Meanwhile, an operational creditor initiated CIRP against tenant at NCLT, and moratorium was declared.', '1. Can a Commercial Court proceed with eviction and recovery of arrears while an IBC Section 14 moratorium is operative?\n2. Does unpaid lease rental constitute operational debt?', NULL, 'The provisions of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code 2016 have overriding effect over other laws pursuant to Section 238. Section 14(1)(a) explicitly prohibits the institution or continuation of suits against the corporate debtor. Furthermore, Section 14(1)(d) prohibits recovery of leased property in possession of the corporate debtor. The Commercial Court must stay its hands.', 'The statutory moratorium under Section 14 of the IBC 2016 operates as an absolute statutory bar against continuing eviction and recovery suits against a corporate debtor in commercial courts.', 'Commercial appeal allowed. Proceedings before the Commercial Court kept in abeyance pending CIRP.', 'Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code 2016 (Section 9, Section 14, Section 238); Commercial Courts Act 2015; Transfer of Property Act 1882 (Section 106).', 'completed'),
